  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $9!=9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!$ and $6!=6\\cdot 5!\\,.$ Then, we can cancel the $7!$ and $5!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!} & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!\\cdot 5!}{7!\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{7!}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{6} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$",
